"Reorders a numeric matrix using hierarchical clustering and shows the resulting group structure with aligned dendrograms.\n\nUse when: Patterns among many rows or columns are not visible in the original ordering. A justified distance metric can express similarity among the measured profiles.\n\nAvoid when: The distance or scaling choices have no defensible domain interpretation.\n\nVisual grammar: Cell color encodes a matrix value. Leaf order encodes clustering arrangement; dendrogram merge height encodes the chosen linkage distance.\n\nBuild: Select and disclose scaling, distance, and linkage methods. Cluster rows and columns as appropriate using the same included matrix. Reorder cells to match leaves and reserve separate gutters for dendrograms and labels.\n\nCheck: Check dendrogram leaves align exactly with matrix rows or columns. Verify missing-value handling occurs before distance calculations. State whether row or column standardization changes the units of the color scale.\n\nPitfalls: Clustering can create visually persuasive groups even in weak structure. Merge heights are not automatically statistical confidence, and reordered adjacency is method-dependent.\n\nExample: Cluster synthetic product preference profiles after declared row standardization, with the standardized-value legend and aligned dendrograms.\n\nAlternatives: Heatmap, Parallel coordinates, Correlation matrix."
